---
name: conformance-literal-pattern-in-case-lambda
---

# A literal pattern must work in a `case` LAMBDA, not only in a `match`

F decides how to lower a set of arms in TWO places: `parseMatchArms` for an explicit `x match { … }`
and `caseLamIsGen` for a `case` lambda (`xs.map { case … }`). They answer the same question and they
must agree.

They stopped agreeing. The char-literal fix (`v2-char-is-an-int`, `df5785f6f`) gave char literals
their own token kind and taught `parseMatchArms` about it via `isNumLitHead` — but not the twin. A
char literal pattern inside a `case` lambda therefore stopped being recognised as a literal arm, fell
through to the constructor path, and died at runtime with `match: no arm for /-1`. An explicit
`match` on the same pattern was fine, which is exactly why a `match`-only case could not see it.

This case gates BOTH walkers on BOTH pattern kinds that route through them — a char literal and the
unit literal `()` — so neither can be fixed while the other silently rots.

```scalascript
def kindL(c: Char): String = List(c).map { case '\n' => "NL" case _ => "other" }.mkString

def kindM(c: Char): String = c match
  case '\n' => "NL"
  case _    => "other"

def unitL(x: Any): String = List(x).map { case () => "UNIT" case _ => "OTHER" }.mkString

def unitM(x: Any): String = x match
  case () => "UNIT"
  case _  => "OTHER"

def main() =
  // char literal — lambda and match must agree with each other and with the golden
  println(kindL('\n'))
  println(kindM('\n'))
  println(kindL('z'))
  println(kindM('z'))

  // unit literal — `()` is the unit value, so a literal pattern on it holds
  println(unitL(()))
  println(unitM(()))
  println(unitL(7))
  println(unitM(7))
```
